sections.body:
An IAM policy is a JSON document describing permissions. Its core shape is a `Version` (almost always the literal string `"2012-10-17"`, a historical policy-language version marker) and a `Statement` array, where each statement has an `Effect` (`"Allow"` or `"Deny"`), an `Action` (one or more service-operation pairs like `s3:GetObject` or `ec2:RunInstances`), and a `Resource` (one or more ARNs the action applies to, which can include wildcards). A minimal example:

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::app-data-bucket/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

This grants whoever the policy is attached to permission to read (`GetObject`) any object inside `app-data-bucket`, and nothing else. There are two broad categories of where a policy can live: **identity-based policies**, attached directly to a user, group, or role (this describes what that principal can do), and **resource-based policies**, attached directly to a resource like an S3 bucket or an IAM role's trust policy (this describes who is allowed to act on that specific resource, regardless of what their own identity-based policies say). Both kinds get evaluated together for any given request that involves a resource capable of having its own resource policy -- which is exactly why an S3 access problem can be caused by either side, and why you often have to check both a user's/role's policies *and* the bucket's own policy to fully explain a given permission outcome.

examples.code:
// Narrow: exactly what a report-reading service needs
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": "s3:GetObject",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::app-data-bucket/*"
  }]
}

// Broad: a common "just get it working" shortcut
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": "s3:*",
    "Resource": "*"
  }]
}

// Trust policy (attached to the role itself): WHO can assume this role
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": { "Service": "ec2.amazonaws.com" },
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
  }]
}

// Permissions policy (attached to the role): WHAT it can do once assumed
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject"],
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::app-uploads-bucket/*"
  }]
}

// Policy A, attached to the role: broad S3 access
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{ "Effect": "Allow", "Action": "s3:*", "Resource": "*" }]
}

// Policy B, also attached to the same role: an explicit safety-net deny
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{ "Effect": "Deny", "Action": "s3:DeleteBucket", "Resource": "*" }]
}

